# Horse Model Loading Performance Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Keep the existing `Models::load()` / `Renderer::ModelScene::instantiate()` API while moving glTF texture decode/derivation off the caller thread and adding a transparent compiled model cache.

**Architecture:** Model geometry and metadata remain synchronously published so current `ModelHandle` semantics stay intact. glTF import registers stable deferred texture handles; a generic internal worker pool decodes/derives textures and performs cache file I/O, while main-thread resource pumping commits completions and advances `Models::resourceRevision()`. Warm loads deserialize normalized Horse model data from the platform cache and rebuild deferred texture handles without reparsing glTF.

**Tech Stack:** C++20, Horse Models/Renderer/ECS systems, SDL_GPU resource fallback, C-BuildSystem, standard-library threads/filesystem/binary I/O.

**Spec:** `MODEL_LOADING_DESIGN`

## Global Constraints

- Horse-only change; do not modify GAME.
- Keep `Models::load(path)` and `Renderer::ModelScene::instantiate(...)` unchanged for callers.
- Keep geometry/model publication synchronous in this phase.
- Worker threads may read files, decode/derive images and write completed cache payloads, but may not mutate ECS, GPU state, model/mesh/material registries or published texture assets.
- Pending/failed textures use existing renderer fallback resources and must not invalidate an otherwise valid model.
- Reuse `Models::resourceRevision()` and shared scene-resource synchronization; no renderer-specific loading system.
- Cache corruption or cache-write failure must never prevent source loading.
- No `docs/superpowers` tree; project docs remain at repository root.

